import{t as e}from"./logicgen-B1mVTMM_.js";import{r as t,t as n}from"./expand-B9sP1tsw.js";var r=[[[3,1,`reasoning`,`Tom is taller than Sam, and Sam is taller than Ben. Who is the shortest?`,`Ben`,[`Tom`,`Sam`,`They are all equal`],`Tom is taller than Sam and Sam is taller than Ben, so Ben is the shortest of the three.`],[3,1,`reasoning`,`Every marble in a bag is blue. Rina takes one out without looking. What colour is it?`,`Blue`,[`Red`,`It could be any colour`,`Green`],`Every marble in the bag is blue, so whichever one comes out must be blue.`],[3,1,`reasoning`,`Which one does not belong: apple, banana, carrot, mango?`,`Carrot`,[`Apple`,`Banana`,`Mango`],`Apple, banana and mango are fruits; a carrot is a vegetable (a root).`],[3,1,`reasoning`,`A red car is in front of a blue car, and the blue car is in front of a green car. Which car is at the back?`,`The green car`,[`The red car`,`The blue car`,`No car is at the back`],`Red leads, blue is behind it, and the green car is behind blue, so the green car is last.`],[3,1,`riddle`,`What has a neck but no head?`,`A bottle`,[`A giraffe`,`A worm`,`A pencil`],`A bottle narrows into a neck at the top, but it has no head.`],[3,1,`riddle`,`What has legs but cannot walk?`,`A table`,[`A spider`,`A dog`,`A frog`],`A table stands on legs, but it never takes a step.`],[3,2,`reasoning`,`If it rains, the playground gets wet. The playground is completely dry. Did it rain this morning?`,`No`,[`Yes`,`Yes, very heavily`,`Only a few drops fell`],`Rain would have made the playground wet. It is dry, so it did not rain.`],[3,2,`reasoning`,`A box holds a red ball, a green ball and a yellow ball in a row. The green ball is on the right. The red ball is not on the left. Where is the red ball?`,`In the middle`,[`On the left`,`On the right`,`Outside the box`],`Green takes the right, and red is not on the left, so red must be in the middle.`],[3,2,`reasoning`,`Which word does not belong: happy, glad, joyful, angry?`,`Angry`,[`Happy`,`Glad`,`Joyful`],`Happy, glad and joyful all mean pleased; angry means the opposite kind of feeling.`],[3,2,`reasoning`,`Kofi stands in front of Lena, and Lena stands in front of Mo. Who is in the middle?`,`Lena`,[`Kofi`,`Mo`,`Nobody`],`The order is Kofi, then Lena, then Mo, so Lena is in the middle.`],[3,2,`riddle`,`What runs but never walks, and has a bed but never sleeps?`,`A river`,[`A horse`,`A clock`,`A baby`],`A river runs (flows) and has a riverbed, but it never walks or sleeps.`],[3,2,`riddle`,`What kind of room has no doors and no windows?`,`A mushroom`,[`A cave`,`A tent`,`A cupboard`],`It is a joke on the word: a mushroom ends in ‘room’ but has no doors or windows.`],[3,3,`reasoning`,`Five children stand in a line, and Arif is third from the front. How many children stand in front of him?`,`2`,[`3`,`1`,`4`],`Third from the front means two children are ahead: the first and the second.`],[3,3,`reasoning`,`Every bird in the aviary can fly. Pip is a bird that lives in the aviary. Which statement must be true?`,`Pip can fly`,[`Pip cannot fly`,`Pip is not a bird`,`Pip lives outside`],`If every bird in the aviary can fly and Pip is one of them, then Pip can fly.`],[3,3,`reasoning`,`A holiday starts on a Tuesday and lasts five days. On which day is the last day of the holiday?`,`Saturday`,[`Sunday`,`Friday`,`Monday`],`Count five days: Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday.`],[3,3,`riddle`,`What goes up and down but never moves?`,`A staircase`,[`A lift`,`A yo-yo`,`A kite`],`Stairs lead up and down, but the staircase itself stays still.`],[3,3,`riddle`,`A girl has three brothers, and each brother has one sister. How many children are in the family?`,`4`,[`6`,`7`,`5`],`All three brothers share the same sister, the girl herself: 3 boys + 1 girl = 4 children.`],[3,2,`reasoning`,`Which is the odd one out: January, March, May, June?`,`June`,[`January`,`March`,`May`],`January, March and May each have 31 days; June has only 30.`],[4,1,`reasoning`,`Lucy is older than Max. Max is older than Nora. Ola is older than Lucy. Who is the oldest?`,`Ola`,[`Lucy`,`Max`,`Nora`],`From oldest to youngest the order is Ola, Lucy, Max, Nora.`],[4,1,`reasoning`,`You face north and turn right, then turn right again. Which way are you facing now?`,`South`,[`East`,`West`,`North`],`One right turn from north faces east; a second right turn faces south.`],[4,1,`reasoning`,`Which is the odd one out: kilometre, metre, kilogram, centimetre?`,`Kilogram`,[`Kilometre`,`Metre`,`Centimetre`],`Kilometre, metre and centimetre measure length; a kilogram measures mass.`],[4,1,`riddle`,`What has a thumb and four fingers but is not alive?`,`A glove`,[`A hand`,`A starfish`,`A fork`],`A glove is shaped like a hand, with a thumb and four fingers, but it is not alive.`],[4,1,`riddle`,`What has words but never speaks?`,`A book`,[`A parrot`,`A radio`,`A teacher`],`A book is full of words, but it cannot say any of them aloud.`],[4,2,`reasoning`,`Ali, Bina and Chandra each own one pet: a cat, a dog or a fish. Ali is allergic to fur. Bina's pet needs a daily walk. Who owns the cat?`,`Chandra`,[`Ali`,`Bina`,`Nobody`],`Ali, allergic to fur, must have the fish; the pet that is walked is the dog (Bina's); so the cat is Chandra's.`],[4,2,`reasoning`,`If the day before yesterday was Thursday, what day is tomorrow?`,`Sunday`,[`Saturday`,`Friday`,`Monday`],`Day before yesterday Thursday → yesterday Friday → today Saturday → tomorrow Sunday.`],[4,2,`reasoning`,`A drawer holds red socks and blue socks mixed together, and it is dark. How many socks must you take to be sure of a matching pair?`,`3`,[`2`,`4`,`5`],`With only two colours, any three socks must include two of the same colour.`],[4,2,`riddle`,`What is black when it is clean and white when it is dirty?`,`A chalkboard`,[`A shirt`,`A cat`,`Snow`],`A clean chalkboard is black; chalk writing makes it look white and dirty.`],[4,2,`riddle`,`What gets bigger the more you take away from it?`,`A hole`,[`A pile`,`A cake`,`A bag`],`Digging takes earth away, and the hole grows.`],[4,2,`riddle`,`Which word is spelled wrong in every dictionary?`,`Wrong`,[`Incorrect`,`Dictionary`,`Spelled`],`It is a word trick: the word ‘wrong’ is always spelled w-r-o-n-g.`],[4,3,`reasoning`,`In a race, you overtake the runner in second place. What place are you in now?`,`Second`,[`First`,`Third`,`Last`],`You take the place of the runner you passed, so you are now second, not first.`],[4,3,`reasoning`,`A farmer has 17 sheep. All but 9 run away. How many sheep are left?`,`9`,[`8`,`17`,`26`],`‘All but 9’ means every sheep except 9 ran off, so 9 remain.`],[4,3,`reasoning`,`Four friends sit in a row. Ana sits at the left end and Ben sits next to her. Cal does not sit next to Ben. Where does Dev sit?`,`Third from the left`,[`Second from the left`,`At the right end`,`At the left end`],`Ana is first and Ben second. Cal cannot be third (next to Ben), so Cal is fourth and Dev is third.`],[4,3,`riddle`,`Mary's father has five daughters: Nana, Nene, Nini and Nono. What is the name of the fifth daughter?`,`Mary`,[`Nunu`,`Nina`,`Nana`],`The puzzle starts by saying they are Mary's father's daughters, so the fifth daughter is Mary.`],[4,3,`riddle`,`What can you keep after giving it to someone?`,`Your word`,[`A gift`,`A coin`,`A ticket`],`When you give your word (a promise), you can still keep it by doing what you said.`],[5,1,`reasoning`,`No reptile has fur. All snakes are reptiles. Which statement follows?`,`No snake has fur`,[`Some snakes have fur`,`All furry animals are snakes`,`Some reptiles are not snakes`],`Snakes are inside the group of reptiles, and nothing in that group has fur, so no snake has fur.`],[5,1,`reasoning`,`Which is longer: one week or 100 hours?`,`One week`,[`100 hours`,`They are equal`,`It depends on the month`],`One week is 7 × 24 = 168 hours, which is more than 100 hours.`],[5,1,`riddle`,`What is so fragile that just saying its name breaks it?`,`Silence`,[`Glass`,`Ice`,`A bubble`],`The moment you say ‘silence’ out loud, the silence is broken.`],[5,1,`riddle`,`What has cities but no houses, forests but no trees, and rivers but no water?`,`A map`,[`A desert`,`A garden`,`A cloud`],`A map shows cities, forests and rivers, but only as drawings.`],[5,2,`reasoning`,`Five runners finish a race. Nila beats Omar, Omar beats Pia, Raj finishes right after Nila, and Sami is last. Who finishes third?`,`Omar`,[`Raj`,`Pia`,`Nila`],`Nila must be first with Raj right behind her; Omar and Pia follow in that order, and Sami is last: Nila, Raj, Omar, Pia, Sami.`],[5,2,`reasoning`,`A month begins on a Friday and has 31 days. On which day does it end?`,`Sunday`,[`Friday`,`Saturday`,`Monday`],`Day 29 is a Friday again (28 days later), so day 30 is Saturday and day 31 is Sunday.`],[5,2,`reasoning`,`Bird is to nest as bee is to …`,`Hive`,[`Honey`,`Flower`,`Wing`],`A nest is the home a bird builds; a hive is the home of bees.`],[5,2,`reasoning`,`A box has 5 red, 4 green and 3 blue balls. How many must you draw without looking to be certain of getting a green one?`,`9`,[`5`,`4`,`12`],`In the worst case you first draw all 5 red and all 3 blue (8 balls); the 9th must be green.`],[5,2,`riddle`,`What goes through towns and fields but never moves?`,`A road`,[`A train`,`A river`,`The wind`],`A road passes through towns and fields while staying exactly where it is.`],[5,2,`riddle`,`What can you break without ever touching it?`,`A promise`,[`A plate`,`A stick`,`A window`],`A promise is broken by not doing what you said, with no touching at all.`],[5,3,`reasoning`,`A snail climbs a 10 m wall. It climbs 3 m each day and slips back 2 m each night. On which day does it reach the top?`,`Day 8`,[`Day 10`,`Day 7`,`Day 9`],`After each full day and night it is 1 m higher, so after 7 nights it is at 7 m; on day 8 it climbs 3 m and reaches 10 m.`],[5,3,`reasoning`,`Six friends each shake hands once with every other friend. How many handshakes take place?`,`15`,[`30`,`36`,`12`],`Each of the 6 shakes 5 hands, giving 30, but every handshake involves two people: 30 ÷ 2 = 15.`],[5,3,`reasoning`,`In a queue, Jun is 5th from the front and 8th from the back. How many people are in the queue?`,`12`,[`13`,`11`,`40`],`There are 4 people ahead of Jun and 7 behind, plus Jun: 4 + 1 + 7 = 12.`],[5,3,`riddle`,`How many times can you subtract 5 from 25?`,`Once`,[`5 times`,`4 times`,`25 times`],`After the first subtraction you are subtracting 5 from 20, not from 25.`],[5,3,`riddle`,`A doctor gives you three pills and tells you to take one every half hour. How long will the pills last?`,`1 hour`,[`1.5 hours`,`2 hours`,`30 minutes`],`Take one now, one half an hour later and the last an hour after the first: the pills last 1 hour.`],[6,1,`reasoning`,`If all bloops are razzies and all razzies are lazzies, must all bloops be lazzies?`,`Yes`,[`No`,`Only some of them`,`It cannot be known`],`Bloops sit inside razzies, which sit inside lazzies, so every bloop is a lazzie.`],[6,1,`reasoning`,`Which is the odd one out: guitar, violin, flute, cello?`,`Flute`,[`Guitar`,`Violin`,`Cello`],`Guitar, violin and cello have strings; a flute is a wind instrument.`],[6,1,`reasoning`,`A glove is to a hand as a sock is to …`,`A foot`,[`A shoe`,`A leg`,`A sandal`],`A glove covers a hand in the same way that a sock covers a foot.`],[6,1,`riddle`,`The more of it there is, the less you can see. What is it?`,`Darkness`,[`Light`,`Glasses`,`Paint`],`As darkness grows, you are able to see less and less.`],[6,2,`reasoning`,`Some cats are black. Everything black soaks up sunlight well. Which statement must be true?`,`Some cats soak up sunlight well`,[`All cats soak up sunlight well`,`No cats soak up sunlight well`,`Every black thing is a cat`],`The black cats are black things, so at least those cats soak up sunlight well; nothing is known about the others.`],[6,2,`reasoning`,`A bat and a ball cost 110 taka together. The bat costs 100 taka more than the ball. How much is the ball?`,`5 taka`,[`10 taka`,`100 taka`,`55 taka`],`If the ball is 5 taka, the bat is 105 taka and together they make 110. A 10-taka ball would make the total 120.`],[6,2,`reasoning`,`Asha, Bilal, Chen and Dana sit at a square table, one to a side. Asha sits opposite Bilal, and Chen sits on Asha's left. Who sits on Asha's right?`,`Dana`,[`Bilal`,`Chen`,`Nobody`],`Opposite and left are taken by Bilal and Chen, so the fourth person, Dana, is on Asha's right.`],[6,2,`riddle`,`What can you hold in your right hand but never in your left hand?`,`Your left elbow`,[`A pencil`,`A spoon`,`Your right knee`],`Your left hand cannot reach round to hold its own elbow, but your right hand can.`],[6,2,`riddle`,`Two fathers and two sons go fishing. Each catches one fish, yet only three fish are caught. How?`,`They are three generations`,[`One fish was thrown back`,`Two of them shared a fish`,`One fish escaped`],`A grandfather, his son and his grandson make two fathers and two sons, but only three people.`],[6,3,`reasoning`,`A lily patch doubles in size every day and covers the whole pond on day 48. On which day did it cover half the pond?`,`Day 47`,[`Day 24`,`Day 46`,`Day 12`],`Because it doubles daily, the day before it covers the whole pond it covers half: day 47.`],[6,3,`reasoning`,`If 5 machines make 5 gadgets in 5 minutes, how long do 100 machines take to make 100 gadgets?`,`5 minutes`,[`100 minutes`,`20 minutes`,`1 minute`],`Each machine makes one gadget in 5 minutes, so 100 machines make 100 gadgets in the same 5 minutes.`],[6,3,`reasoning`,`In a class of 30, 18 like tea, 15 like coffee and 5 like neither. How many like both?`,`8`,[`3`,`5`,`13`],`25 pupils like at least one drink. 18 + 15 = 33 counts the both-group twice, so both = 33 − 25 = 8.`],[6,3,`riddle`,`A brick weighs 1 kg plus half a brick. How much does a whole brick weigh?`,`2 kg`,[`1.5 kg`,`1 kg`,`3 kg`],`If half a brick plus 1 kg makes a whole brick, the other half must be 1 kg, so the brick weighs 2 kg.`],[6,3,`reasoning`,`Exactly one of three boxes holds a prize, and exactly one label is true. Box 1: ‘The prize is here.’ Box 2: ‘The prize is not here.’ Box 3: ‘The prize is not in Box 1.’ Where is the prize?`,`Box 2`,[`Box 1`,`Box 3`,`In none of them`],`Prize in Box 2 makes only label 3 true. In Box 1, labels 1 and 2 are both true; in Box 3, labels 2 and 3 are both true.`],[7,1,`reasoning`,`All roses are flowers, and some flowers fade quickly. Does it follow that some roses fade quickly?`,`No, not necessarily`,[`Yes, always`,`Yes, all roses do`,`No roses ever fade`],`The flowers that fade quickly might all be other kinds of flower, so nothing certain follows about roses.`],[7,1,`reasoning`,`If you water the plant, it grows. The plant grew. Can you be sure it was watered?`,`No`,[`Yes`,`Yes, at least twice`,`Only if it was sunny`],`Something else, such as rain, could have made it grow. Reasoning backwards like this is a common mistake.`],[7,1,`reasoning`,`Only members may enter the club. Jamal entered the club. Which conclusion is valid?`,`Jamal is a member`,[`Jamal is not a member`,`Every member entered`,`Jamal runs the club`],`If only members may enter, anyone who entered must be a member.`],[7,1,`riddle`,`A rooster lays an egg on the peak of a sloping roof. Which side does the egg roll down?`,`Roosters don't lay eggs`,[`The left side`,`The right side`,`The steeper side`],`Roosters are male chickens, and only hens lay eggs.`],[7,2,`reasoning`,`Two days ago, Nabil said, ‘The day after tomorrow is Saturday.’ What day is it today?`,`Saturday`,[`Thursday`,`Monday`,`Friday`],`When Nabil spoke it was Thursday (two days before Saturday). Two days after that is today: Saturday.`],[7,2,`reasoning`,`On an island, knights always tell the truth and knaves always lie. Ava says, ‘I am a knave.’ What can you conclude?`,`Nobody there could say that`,[`Ava must be a knight`,`Ava must be a knave`,`Ava tells the truth`],`A knight would be lying and a knave would be telling the truth, which neither can do.`],[7,2,`reasoning`,`In two years, Lina will be twice as old as she was five years ago. How old is Lina now?`,`12`,[`7`,`10`,`14`],`In two years she is 14, and five years ago she was 7: 14 is twice 7.`],[7,2,`riddle`,`A boy and his father are hurt in a crash. The surgeon says, ‘I cannot operate on this boy: he is my son.’ Who is the surgeon?`,`The boy's mother`,[`The boy's uncle`,`A stranger`,`The boy's brother`],`The surgeon is his mother. The riddle only works if you wrongly assume surgeons are men.`],[7,2,`reasoning`,`Which word cannot be made from the letters of TEACHER?`,`CHAIR`,[`CHEAT`,`REACH`,`THERE`],`TEACHER has no letter I, so CHAIR cannot be made. THERE uses the two Es that TEACHER has.`],[7,3,`reasoning`,`In a normal (non-leap) year, 1 January is a Monday. What day is 31 December?`,`Monday`,[`Tuesday`,`Sunday`,`Saturday`],`31 December is 364 days after 1 January, which is exactly 52 weeks, so it is also a Monday.`],[7,3,`reasoning`,`You have 9 identical-looking coins, and one is heavier. What is the fewest number of balance weighings that always finds it?`,`2`,[`3`,`4`,`1`],`Weigh 3 against 3: the heavy coin is in the heavier pan or in the 3 left out. Then weigh 1 against 1 from that group.`],[7,3,`reasoning`,`Leo says, ‘Mia is a knave.’ Mia says, ‘Leo and I are both knights.’ Knights always tell the truth and knaves always lie. Who is the knight?`,`Leo`,[`Mia`,`Leo and Mia alike`,`Neither Leo nor Mia`],`If Mia were a knight, Leo would be one too, yet he calls her a knave. So Mia lies, and Leo's statement is true: Leo is the knight.`],[7,3,`riddle`,`A man looks at a portrait and says, ‘Brothers and sisters I have none, but that man's father is my father's son.’ Who is in the portrait?`,`His son`,[`He himself`,`His father`,`His nephew`],`With no brothers, ‘my father's son’ is the speaker himself, so the man in the portrait is his son.`],[7,3,`reasoning`,`Three switches outside a closed room each work one of three bulbs inside. You may go in only once. What lets you match every switch?`,`Feel which dark bulb is warm`,[`Flick all three at once`,`Count the bulbs`,`Peek under the door`],`Turn switch 1 on for ten minutes, then off, and turn switch 2 on. Inside: lit bulb = 2, warm dark bulb = 1, cold dark bulb = 3.`],[8,1,`reasoning`,`Which statement means the same as ‘If an animal is a fish, it lives in water’?`,`If it doesn't live in water, it isn't a fish`,[`If it lives in water, it is a fish`,`If it isn't a fish, it doesn't live in water`,`Every water animal is a fish`],`This is the contrapositive, which always has the same truth as the original. The other two reversals do not.`],[8,1,`reasoning`,`‘Every statement in this box is false’ is written alone in a box. This kind of self-contradiction is called a …`,`Paradox`,[`Proverb`,`Metaphor`,`Syllogism`],`If the sentence is true it must be false, and if false it must be true: a paradox.`],[8,1,`reasoning`,`Two fair coins are tossed. Which result is most likely?`,`One head and one tail`,[`Two heads`,`Two tails`,`All three are equally likely`],`There are four equal outcomes: HH, HT, TH, TT. One of each happens two ways, so it has a chance of 1/2.`],[8,2,`reasoning`,`A cube is painted red all over and then cut into 27 equal small cubes. How many small cubes have no paint on them?`,`1`,[`0`,`8`,`6`],`Only the single cube right in the centre of the 3 × 3 × 3 block never touched the outside.`],[8,2,`reasoning`,`In a group of 13 people, which of these must be true?`,`At least two share a birth month`,[`Two share a birthday`,`Everyone has a different birth month`,`Three share a birth month`],`There are only 12 months, so 13 people cannot all have different birth months.`],[8,2,`reasoning`,`A book's pages are numbered from 1 to 100. How many times is the digit 9 printed?`,`20`,[`10`,`19`,`11`],`9 appears in the ones place 10 times (9, 19, … 99) and in the tens place 10 times (90 to 99): 20 in all.`],[8,2,`reasoning`,`Rearrange the letters of LISTEN to make a word meaning ‘quiet’.`,`SILENT`,[`TINSEL`,`ENLIST`,`INLETS`],`All four are anagrams of LISTEN, but only SILENT means quiet.`],[8,3,`reasoning`,`Exactly 100 days after a Sunday, what day of the week is it?`,`Tuesday`,[`Sunday`,`Monday`,`Thursday`],`100 = 14 weeks (98 days) + 2 days, so it is two days after Sunday: Tuesday.`],[8,3,`reasoning`,`A square has both of its diagonals drawn. How many triangles of any size can you find?`,`8`,[`4`,`6`,`10`],`The diagonals make 4 small triangles; each pair of neighbouring small triangles also forms a larger one, giving 4 more.`],[8,3,`reasoning`,`Sara is twice as old as her brother. In five years their ages will add up to 40. How old is her brother now?`,`10`,[`15`,`20`,`8`],`Brother b, Sara 2b: (2b + 5) + (b + 5) = 40, so 3b = 30 and b = 10.`],[8,3,`riddle`,`A boy points to a photo: ‘Her mother is my mother's only daughter.’ Who is in the photo, if the boy has one sister?`,`His niece`,[`His sister`,`His daughter`,`His cousin`],`His mother's only daughter is his sister, so the girl in the photo is his sister's daughter: his niece.`]],[[3,1,`reasoning`,`Puppy is to dog as kitten is to …`,`Cat`,[`Mouse`,`Cow`,`Lamb`],`A puppy grows into a dog, and a kitten grows into a cat.`],[3,1,`reasoning`,`Which comes next: small, medium, …?`,`Large`,[`Tiny`,`Smaller`,`Short`],`The sizes grow step by step: small, medium, large.`],[3,1,`riddle`,`What do you call a bear with no teeth?`,`A gummy bear`,[`A teddy bear`,`A polar bear`,`A brown bear`],`It is a joke: without teeth it can only chew with its gums, so it is a ‘gummy’ bear.`],[3,1,`riddle`,`What has four wheels and flies?`,`A rubbish truck`,[`An aeroplane`,`A helicopter`,`A kite`],`A rubbish truck has four wheels, and flies (the insects) buzz around it.`],[3,1,`riddle`,`What has lots of eyes but cannot see?`,`A potato`,[`A spider`,`A peacock`,`An owl`],`The little buds on a potato's skin are called eyes, but a potato cannot see.`],[3,2,`reasoning`,`Which day is two days after Saturday?`,`Monday`,[`Sunday`,`Tuesday`,`Friday`],`One day after Saturday is Sunday; two days after is Monday.`],[3,2,`reasoning`,`Sami is wearing a hat. Everyone wearing a hat is going to the fair. Is Sami going to the fair?`,`Yes`,[`No`,`Maybe not`,`Only if it is sunny`],`Everyone with a hat is going, and Sami has a hat, so Sami is going.`],[3,2,`reasoning`,`Which number is the odd one out: 4, 8, 11, 12?`,`11`,[`4`,`8`,`12`],`4, 8 and 12 are even numbers in the 4 times table; 11 is odd.`],[3,2,`riddle`,`What starts with E, ends with E, and usually holds only one letter?`,`An envelope`,[`An eagle`,`An engine`,`An eye`],`An envelope begins and ends with e, and it holds a letter (a message).`],[3,2,`reasoning`,`Which is heavier: a kilogram of cotton or a kilogram of stones?`,`They weigh the same`,[`The stones`,`The cotton`,`It depends on the weather`],`They weigh the same: a kilogram is a kilogram. The stones simply take up less space.`],[3,3,`reasoning`,`A ball is in one of three boxes: small, medium and large. It is not in the small box, and it is not in the largest box. Where is it?`,`In the medium box`,[`In the small box`,`In the large box`,`In no box at all`],`Only the medium box is left once the small and the large boxes are ruled out.`],[3,3,`reasoning`,`Lily plays outside only on dry days. Lily is playing outside today. What is the weather like?`,`It is dry`,[`It is raining`,`It is snowing`,`It is stormy`],`Lily plays outside only when it is dry, so today must be a dry day.`],[3,3,`riddle`,`What has many rings but no fingers?`,`A tree`,[`A snake`,`A fish`,`A rope`],`A tree trunk grows one new ring every year; you can count them on a cut stump.`],[4,1,`reasoning`,`A thermometer is to temperature as a ruler is to …`,`Length`,[`Weight`,`Time`,`Pencil`],`A thermometer measures temperature; a ruler measures length.`],[4,1,`reasoning`,`A shoe is to a foot as a hat is to …`,`A head`,[`Hair`,`A hand`,`A coat`],`You wear a shoe on your foot and a hat on your head.`],[4,1,`riddle`,`I have branches, but no fruit, no trunk and no leaves. What am I?`,`A bank`,[`A tree`,`A bush`,`A vine`],`A bank has branches (its offices in different places) but no trunk, fruit or leaves.`],[4,1,`riddle`,`What can you put in a bucket to make it lighter?`,`A hole`,[`A feather`,`A balloon`,`Some air`],`A hole lets the water run out, so the bucket gets lighter.`],[4,2,`reasoning`,`Nadia is older than Omar but younger than Priya. Who is the youngest?`,`Omar`,[`Nadia`,`Priya`,`They are the same age`],`Priya is older than Nadia, who is older than Omar, so Omar is the youngest.`],[4,2,`reasoning`,`A clock shows 9 o'clock. What time will it show 5 hours later?`,`2 o'clock`,[`14 o'clock`,`4 o'clock`,`1 o'clock`],`Count on 5 hours from 9: 10, 11, 12, 1, 2. A clock face starts again after 12.`],[4,2,`reasoning`,`A, B and C run a race. A is not first, and C is last. Who won?`,`B`,[`A`,`C`,`Nobody`],`C is last and A is not first, so B must have come first.`],[4,2,`reasoning`,`A snail is behind a beetle and in front of an ant. Which creature is last?`,`The ant`,[`The snail`,`The beetle`,`They are side by side`],`The order is beetle, snail, ant, so the ant comes last.`],[4,2,`riddle`,`What has a head, a foot and four legs?`,`A bed`,[`A horse`,`A chair`,`A table`],`A bed has a head end, a foot end and four legs.`],[4,2,`riddle`,`What comes once in a minute, twice in a moment, but never in a thousand years?`,`The letter m`,[`The second hand`,`A blink`,`A heartbeat`],`Count the letter m: one in ‘minute’, two in ‘moment’, none in ‘a thousand years’.`],[4,3,`reasoning`,`Three friends each like a different fruit: apples, grapes or plums. Ria likes neither apples nor grapes. Joy does not like apples. What does Joy like?`,`Grapes`,[`Apples`,`Plums`,`Nothing`],`Ria must like plums. Joy does not like apples, so Joy likes grapes, and apples go to the third friend.`],[4,3,`reasoning`,`Six cups stand in a row: three full, then three empty. Moving only one cup, how can you make them alternate full and empty?`,`Pour cup 2 into cup 5`,[`Swap the first and last cups`,`Move cup 3 to the end`,`Turn every cup over`],`Pour cup 2 into cup 5 and put it back: the row reads full, empty, full, empty, full, empty.`],[4,3,`reasoning`,`Counting from 1 to 50, how many numbers end in the digit 7?`,`5`,[`4`,`6`,`7`],`7, 17, 27, 37 and 47: five numbers.`],[4,3,`riddle`,`I am an odd number. Take away one letter and I become even. What number am I?`,`Seven`,[`Nine`,`Three`,`Eleven`],`Take the s off ‘seven’ and you are left with ‘even’.`],[5,1,`reasoning`,`A fish is to water as a bird is to …`,`Air`,[`A nest`,`A feather`,`A tree`],`Fish move through water; birds move through the air.`],[5,1,`reasoning`,`No cat barks, and Tiger is a cat. Which must be true?`,`Tiger does not bark`,[`Tiger barks`,`Tiger is a dog`,`Some cats bark`],`Tiger belongs to the cats, and no cat barks, so Tiger does not bark.`],[5,1,`reasoning`,`Which comes next: one, three, five, seven, …?`,`Nine`,[`Eight`,`Ten`,`Eleven`],`These are the odd numbers, going up by two each time.`],[5,1,`riddle`,`What has a spine but no bones?`,`A book`,[`A fish`,`A cactus`,`A ladder`],`The spine of a book is the edge that holds the pages together.`],[5,1,`riddle`,`What has many needles but never sews?`,`A pine tree`,[`A tailor`,`A sewing machine`,`A dressmaker`],`A pine tree's thin leaves are called needles, but it never sews anything.`],[5,2,`reasoning`,`A train leaves every 15 minutes, starting at 7:00. Which of these times is NOT a departure time?`,`7:50`,[`7:45`,`8:15`,`9:30`],`Departures are at 7:00, 7:15, 7:30, 7:45, 8:00 and so on. 7:50 is not on the list, because 50 is not a multiple of 15.`],[5,2,`reasoning`,`You face east and then turn round to face the opposite way. Which way are you facing now?`,`West`,[`North`,`South`,`East`],`Turning to face the opposite way is a half turn, and the opposite of east is west.`],[5,2,`reasoning`,`A ribbon is cut into 4 pieces with straight cuts across it. How many cuts were made?`,`3`,[`4`,`5`,`2`],`Each cut adds one piece: 1 piece becomes 2, then 3, then 4 after three cuts.`],[5,2,`reasoning`,`A straight fence is 20 m long, with a post every 5 m including both ends. How many posts are there?`,`5`,[`4`,`6`,`20`],`Posts stand at 0, 5, 10, 15 and 20 m: five posts for four gaps.`],[5,2,`reasoning`,`Every Tuesday, Rafi has football practice. Today Rafi has no practice. What can you be sure of?`,`Today is not Tuesday`,[`Today is Wednesday`,`Rafi is unwell`,`Rafi dislikes football`],`If today were Tuesday there would be practice. There is none, so today is not Tuesday.`],[5,2,`riddle`,`What gets sharper the more you use it?`,`Your brain`,[`A knife`,`A pencil`,`Scissors`],`Knives and pencils get blunter with use, but a well-used mind gets sharper.`],[5,3,`reasoning`,`Ann, Bo, Cy and Di sit in a row. Bo sits between Ann and Cy. Di is at the right end, next to Ann. Who is at the left end?`,`Cy`,[`Ann`,`Bo`,`Di`],`Di is 4th and Ann 3rd. Bo sits between Ann and Cy, so Bo is 2nd and Cy is 1st.`],[5,3,`reasoning`,`A clock takes 5 seconds to strike 6 o'clock. How long does it take to strike 12 o'clock?`,`11 seconds`,[`10 seconds`,`12 seconds`,`24 seconds`],`Six strikes have 5 gaps of 1 second each; twelve strikes have 11 gaps, so 11 seconds.`],[5,3,`reasoning`,`A mother is 4 times as old as her son. In 20 years she will be twice as old as him. How old is the son now?`,`10`,[`5`,`20`,`15`],`Son s, mother 4s: 4s + 20 = 2(s + 20), so 4s + 20 = 2s + 40 and s = 10. She is 40 now.`],[5,3,`reasoning`,`Sumi's birthday is the day after tomorrow, and the day after the birthday is Saturday. On which day is the birthday?`,`Friday`,[`Thursday`,`Saturday`,`Wednesday`],`The day after the birthday is Saturday, so the birthday falls on Friday.`],[5,3,`riddle`,`I am a three-digit number. My tens digit is 5 more than my ones digit, and my hundreds digit is 8 less than my tens digit. What am I?`,`194`,[`149`,`941`,`295`],`The hundreds digit is at least 1, so the tens digit is 9 and the hundreds 1; the ones digit is 9 − 5 = 4.`],[6,1,`reasoning`,`An author is to a novel as a composer is to …`,`A symphony`,[`A painting`,`A poem`,`A statue`],`An author creates a novel; a composer creates music, such as a symphony.`],[6,1,`reasoning`,`Which is the odd one out: square, triangle, cube, circle?`,`Cube`,[`Square`,`Triangle`,`Circle`],`A square, a triangle and a circle are flat shapes; a cube is solid (3-D).`],[6,1,`reasoning`,`A teacher is to a school as a doctor is to …`,`A hospital`,[`Medicine`,`A patient`,`A nurse`],`A teacher works in a school; a doctor works in a hospital.`],[6,1,`riddle`,`What has 13 hearts but no other organs?`,`A pack of cards`,[`A hospital`,`An octopus`,`A lettuce`],`A standard pack of 52 cards has 13 cards in the hearts suit.`],[6,2,`reasoning`,`Some members of the chess club also play football. All chess club members wear badges. Which must be true?`,`Some badge wearers play football`,[`All football players wear badges`,`No football players wear badges`,`All badge wearers play chess`],`The members who play football also wear badges, so at least some badge wearers play football.`],[6,2,`reasoning`,`A, B, C and D each have a different number of marbles. A has more than B. C has fewer than D but more than A. Who has the most?`,`D`,[`A`,`B`,`C`],`From most to fewest the order is D, C, A, B.`],[6,2,`reasoning`,`What is the smallest number of colours needed so that no two neighbouring stripes on a 7-stripe flag share a colour?`,`2`,[`7`,`3`,`4`],`Alternate two colours: 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1. Neighbouring stripes always differ.`],[6,2,`reasoning`,`How many squares of any size are there on a 2 × 2 grid?`,`5`,[`4`,`6`,`8`],`There are 4 small squares plus 1 big square made of all four: 5.`],[6,2,`riddle`,`Forwards I am something you cook in; backwards I am a short sleep. What word am I?`,`Pan`,[`Pot`,`Wok`,`Tin`],`‘Pan’ spelled backwards is ‘nap’, a short sleep.`],[6,3,`reasoning`,`People named Baker, Smith and Taylor work as a baker, a smith and a tailor, but nobody's job matches their name. Smith is not the baker. Who is the tailor?`,`Smith`,[`Baker`,`Taylor`,`It cannot be worked out`],`Neither Baker nor Smith can be the baker, so Taylor bakes. Smith cannot be the smith, so Smith is the tailor.`],[6,3,`reasoning`,`Ten coins lie tails up. Each move turns over exactly two coins. What is the fewest moves needed to have exactly one head showing?`,`It is impossible`,[`1`,`5`,`9`],`Each move changes the number of heads by −2, 0 or +2, so it stays even. One head is odd, so it can never happen.`],[6,3,`reasoning`,`Two trains 100 km apart head towards each other on the same line, each at 50 km/h. How long until they meet?`,`1 hour`,[`2 hours`,`30 minutes`,`4 hours`],`They close the gap at 50 + 50 = 100 km each hour, so they meet after 1 hour.`],[6,3,`reasoning`,`How many times a day do the hour and minute hands of a clock point in exactly the same direction?`,`22`,[`24`,`12`,`11`],`In 12 hours the hands line up 11 times, so in 24 hours they do so 22 times.`],[6,3,`riddle`,`What is the next letter in the series O, T, T, F, F, S, S, …?`,`E`,[`N`,`T`,`O`],`They are the first letters of One, Two, Three, Four, Five, Six, Seven, so next comes Eight.`],[7,1,`reasoning`,`Hunger is to food as thirst is to …`,`Water`,[`Bread`,`Sleep`,`Heat`],`Food ends hunger; water ends thirst.`],[7,1,`reasoning`,`The sentence ‘All the doors are locked’ is false. Which must be true?`,`At least one door is unlocked`,[`Every door is unlocked`,`No door is locked`,`Exactly one door is unlocked`],`The opposite of ‘all are locked’ is ‘at least one is not locked’; it does not mean that every door is open.`],[7,1,`reasoning`,`Which word is the odd one out: whisper, mutter, shout, murmur?`,`Shout`,[`Whisper`,`Mutter`,`Murmur`],`Whisper, mutter and murmur are ways of speaking quietly; to shout is to speak loudly.`],[7,1,`riddle`,`What occurs twice in a week, once in a year, but never in a day?`,`The letter e`,[`A weekend`,`A holiday`,`A sunrise`],`‘Week’ has two e's, ‘year’ has one, and ‘day’ has none.`],[7,1,`riddle`,`Which letter of the alphabet holds the most water?`,`C`,[`W`,`O`,`B`],`It is a play on sounds: the letter C sounds like ‘sea’.`],[7,2,`reasoning`,`In a code, MONDAY is written as NPOEBZ. How is FRIDAY written in the same code?`,`GSJEBZ`,[`GSJECA`,`ESHCZX`,`GRJEBZ`],`Each letter moves one place on in the alphabet: F→G, R→S, I→J, D→E, A→B, Y→Z.`],[7,2,`reasoning`,`In a message, each letter was replaced by the next letter of the alphabet. The message reads IBQQZ. What was the original word?`,`HAPPY`,[`JCRRA`,`GZOOX`,`HOPPY`],`Move each letter back one place: I→H, B→A, Q→P, Q→P, Z→Y.`],[7,2,`reasoning`,`If A = 1, B = 2, C = 3 and so on, what do the letters of BAD add up to?`,`7`,[`6`,`8`,`214`],`B + A + D = 2 + 1 + 4 = 7.`],[7,2,`reasoning`,`One tap fills a tank in 6 hours and a second tap fills it in 3 hours. How long do both taps take together?`,`2 hours`,[`4.5 hours`,`9 hours`,`3 hours`],`Together they fill 1/6 + 1/3 = 1/2 of the tank each hour, so the whole tank takes 2 hours.`],[7,2,`reasoning`,`A clock shows 3:15. What is the angle between the hour hand and the minute hand?`,`7.5°`,[`0°`,`15°`,`90°`],`The minute hand points at 3, but the hour hand has moved a quarter of the way from 3 to 4: a quarter of 30° is 7.5°.`],[7,2,`riddle`,`What can be cracked, made, told and played?`,`A joke`,[`An egg`,`A game`,`A code`],`You can crack a joke, make a joke, tell a joke and play a joke on someone.`],[7,3,`reasoning`,`A farmer must ferry a wolf, a goat and a cabbage across a river; the boat holds the farmer and one of them. Which must cross first?`,`The goat`,[`The wolf`,`The cabbage`,`Nothing`],`Left alone, the wolf would eat the goat and the goat would eat the cabbage. Taking the goat first leaves a safe pair behind.`],[7,3,`reasoning`,`Twelve players enter a knockout tennis tournament, and each match knocks out one player. How many matches are played to find the winner?`,`11`,[`12`,`6`,`24`],`Every player except the winner must lose exactly once, and each match makes one loser: 12 − 1 = 11.`],[7,3,`reasoning`,`What is the smallest whole number greater than 1 that leaves remainder 1 when divided by 2, 3, 4, 5 or 6?`,`61`,[`31`,`121`,`59`],`The lowest common multiple of 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6 is 60, so the number is 60 + 1 = 61.`],[7,3,`riddle`,`How can you drop a raw egg onto a concrete floor without cracking the concrete?`,`Any way you like`,[`Wrap the egg in cloth`,`Drop it from low down`,`Freeze the egg first`],`Concrete is much harder than an egg: the floor will not crack, whatever happens to the egg.`],[8,1,`reasoning`,`‘If a shape is a square, it has four sides.’ Which of these is the converse?`,`If it has four sides, it is a square`,[`If it isn't a square, it hasn't four sides`,`If it hasn't four sides, it isn't a square`,`A square has four sides only on paper`],`The converse swaps the two parts. Here it is false: a rectangle has four sides but need not be a square.`],[8,1,`reasoning`,`What is the logical negation of ‘Some birds cannot fly’?`,`Every bird can fly`,[`No bird can fly`,`Some birds can fly`,`Most birds cannot fly`],`‘Some cannot’ is false only when no bird is unable to fly, that is, when every bird can fly.`],[8,1,`reasoning`,`An argument whose conclusion must be true whenever its premises are true is called …`,`Valid`,[`Circular`,`Biased`,`Anecdotal`],`Validity is about form: true premises guarantee a true conclusion. A sound argument is valid and has true premises too.`],[8,1,`reasoning`,`Proof that a suspect was somewhere else when a crime took place is called …`,`An alibi`,[`A motive`,`A verdict`,`A rumour`],`An alibi shows a person was elsewhere at the time, so they could not have done it.`],[8,1,`riddle`,`What can be seen in the middle of March and April but not at the start or end of either month?`,`The letter r`,[`A full moon`,`A holiday`,`Spring rain`],`MaRch and ApRil both have an r in the middle, but neither word starts or ends with one.`],[8,2,`reasoning`,`A fair die is rolled twice. What is the probability that both rolls show a 6?`,`1/36`,[`1/12`,`1/6`,`2/6`],`Each roll has a 1/6 chance, and the rolls do not affect each other: 1/6 × 1/6 = 1/36.`],[8,2,`reasoning`,`Five runners finish a race with no ties. In how many different orders can they finish?`,`120`,[`25`,`5`,`60`],`5 choices for first, then 4, 3, 2 and 1: 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 120.`],[8,2,`reasoning`,`Ena answered all 40 questions on a test and got 3 times as many right as wrong. How many did Ena get right?`,`30`,[`10`,`27`,`36`],`Right = 3 × wrong and right + wrong = 40, so 4 × wrong = 40: 10 wrong and 30 right.`],[8,2,`riddle`,`Mo takes a shot of a friend, holds it under water for five minutes, then hangs it up to dry. Later the two of them have lunch together. How?`,`Mo developed a photograph`,[`The friend can breathe under water`,`It all happened in a dream`,`They are both actors`],`Mo took a photo (a shot) and developed the film print in a water bath before hanging it to dry.`],[8,3,`reasoning`,`Three boxes hold apples, oranges, and a mix of both, but every label is wrong. You may take one fruit from one box. Which box should you choose?`,`The one labelled ‘mixed’`,[`The one labelled ‘apples’`,`The one labelled ‘oranges’`,`Any box will do`],`The ‘mixed’ box really holds one kind only, so one fruit names it; the other two labels then follow.`],[8,3,`reasoning`,`A number is doubled and then 6 is added. The result is 4 more than three times the number. What is the number?`,`2`,[`6`,`10`,`4`],`2x + 6 = 3x + 4 gives x = 2. Check: 2 × 2 + 6 = 10 and 3 × 2 + 4 = 10.`],[8,3,`reasoning`,`What is the smallest number of people needed to be sure that at least three of them share a birth month?`,`25`,[`13`,`24`,`36`],`With 24 people each month could have exactly two. The 25th person must make three in some month.`],[8,3,`reasoning`,`Three friends pay 30 taka for a room, 10 each. The manager returns 5; the porter keeps 2 and gives 1 back to each friend. Each paid 9 (27 in all), plus the porter's 2 makes 29. Where is the missing taka?`,`Nothing is missing`,[`The porter has it`,`The manager has it`,`It fell on the floor`],`The 27 already includes the porter's 2 (25 for the room + 2). Adding the 2 again is the mistake: 27 + 3 returned = 30.`],[8,3,`riddle`,`The day before two days after the day before tomorrow is Saturday. What day is it today?`,`Friday`,[`Saturday`,`Thursday`,`Sunday`],`The day before tomorrow is today; two days after that is today + 2; the day before that is today + 1 = Saturday, so today is Friday.`]],[[3,1,`reasoning`,`Which is the odd one out: car, bus, bicycle, banana?`,`Banana`,[`Car`,`Bus`,`Bicycle`],`A car, a bus and a bicycle are ways to travel; a banana is a fruit.`],[3,1,`reasoning`,`Which is the odd one out: hat, scarf, gloves, spoon?`,`Spoon`,[`Hat`,`Scarf`,`Gloves`],`A hat, a scarf and gloves are things you wear; a spoon is for eating.`],[3,1,`riddle`,`What has bark but never bites?`,`A tree`,[`A dog`,`A puppy`,`A fox`],`The rough outer layer of a tree trunk is called bark.`],[3,1,`riddle`,`What has a horn but does not honk?`,`A rhinoceros`,[`A car`,`A bus`,`A lorry`],`Rhinos have horns made of keratin, the same stuff as your nails.`],[3,2,`reasoning`,`A box has 3 red pens and 2 blue pens. If you take out every red pen, which colour is left?`,`Blue`,[`Red`,`Green`,`No pens are left`],`Only the 2 blue pens remain in the box.`],[3,2,`reasoning`,`Today is Wednesday. What day was it yesterday?`,`Tuesday`,[`Thursday`,`Monday`,`Friday`],`The day before Wednesday is Tuesday.`],[3,2,`riddle`,`What has ears but cannot hear?`,`A maize plant`,[`A rabbit`,`A dog`,`A cat`],`The cobs on a maize plant are called ears.`],[3,2,`reasoning`,`Which is heavier: a big balloon full of air or a small stone?`,`The small stone`,[`The big balloon`,`They weigh the same`,`Neither weighs anything`],`Size is not the same as weight; the stone packs much more mass into a small space.`],[3,3,`reasoning`,`Five birds sit on a fence. A loud bang scares 2 of them away, and then 1 comes back. How many birds are on the fence now?`,`4`,[`3`,`2`,`8`],`5 − 2 = 3, and then 3 + 1 = 4.`],[3,3,`reasoning`,`A pattern goes circle, square, circle, square, … What is the 7th shape?`,`Circle`,[`Square`,`Triangle`,`Star`],`Odd positions are circles and even positions are squares, and 7 is odd.`],[3,3,`riddle`,`What runs all the way round a garden but never moves?`,`A fence`,[`A dog`,`A hose`,`A cat`],`A fence goes all the way round, but it stays in one place.`],[4,1,`reasoning`,`Which of these words can be made by rearranging the letters of EAT?`,`TEA`,[`TOE`,`TEN`,`TAN`],`TEA uses exactly the letters E, A and T; the others need an O or an N.`],[4,1,`reasoning`,`Lina has 2 sisters and 1 brother. How many children are in Lina's family?`,`4`,[`3`,`5`,`2`],`Lina plus 2 sisters plus 1 brother makes 4 children.`],[4,1,`riddle`,`What kind of tree can you carry in your hand?`,`A palm`,[`An oak`,`A pine`,`A mango tree`],`The palm of your hand is a joke on the palm tree.`],[4,2,`reasoning`,`A clock's minute hand points at the 6. How many minutes past the hour is it?`,`30`,[`6`,`60`,`15`],`For the minute hand, each number on the clock is 5 minutes: 6 × 5 = 30.`],[4,2,`reasoning`,`A triangle has one line drawn from its top corner to the middle of its bottom side. How many triangles can you see?`,`3`,[`2`,`1`,`4`],`The two halves are triangles, and so is the whole shape: 3.`],[4,2,`riddle`,`What kind of coat is best put on wet?`,`A coat of paint`,[`A raincoat`,`A fur coat`,`A winter coat`],`It is a word play: a coat of paint goes on wet and dries afterwards.`],[4,3,`reasoning`,`Three friends share 12 marbles so that each gets 2 more than the friend before. How many does the first friend get?`,`2`,[`4`,`3`,`6`],`The shares are 2, 4 and 6, which add to 12.`],[4,3,`reasoning`,`A 12-hour digital clock shows 12:59. What will it show one minute later?`,`1:00`,[`13:00`,`12:60`,`12:00`],`After 59 minutes the hour moves on, and on a 12-hour clock 12 is followed by 1.`],[4,3,`reasoning`,`Which letter is the fifth letter after C in the alphabet?`,`H`,[`G`,`I`,`F`],`Counting on from C: D, E, F, G, H.`],[4,3,`riddle`,`A man stood in the rain with no umbrella or hat, yet not one hair on his head got wet. How?`,`He was bald`,[`He ran very fast`,`It was only drizzle`,`He stood very still`],`With no hair on his head, no hair could get wet.`],[5,1,`reasoning`,`Which is the odd one out: copper, iron, wood, silver?`,`Wood`,[`Copper`,`Iron`,`Silver`],`Copper, iron and silver are metals; wood is not.`],[5,1,`reasoning`,`Which is the odd one out: kitten, puppy, calf, horse?`,`Horse`,[`Kitten`,`Puppy`,`Calf`],`A kitten, a puppy and a calf are young animals; a horse is grown up (its young is a foal).`],[5,1,`riddle`,`What is always coming but never arrives?`,`Tomorrow`,[`Yesterday`,`Today`,`Midnight`],`When tomorrow comes, it has become today, so tomorrow never actually arrives.`],[5,2,`reasoning`,`A ladder has 10 rungs. You stand on the 4th rung from the bottom and climb up 3 rungs. Which rung are you on?`,`The 7th`,[`The 3rd`,`The 6th`,`The 10th`],`4 + 3 = 7: the seventh rung from the bottom.`],[5,2,`reasoning`,`A family photo shows 2 parents, their 3 children and 1 grandmother. How many generations are in the photo?`,`3`,[`2`,`6`,`4`],`Grandmother, parents and children make three generations.`],[5,2,`reasoning`,`Which of these words can be typed using only the top row of letters on a keyboard (Q W E R T Y U I O P)?`,`TYPEWRITER`,[`KEYBOARD`,`COMPUTER`,`PRINTER`],`Every letter of TYPEWRITER is on the top row; the others need letters such as K, M or N.`],[5,2,`reasoning`,`How many zeros are at the end of 10 × 20 × 30?`,`3`,[`2`,`4`,`6`],`10 × 20 × 30 = 6,000, which ends in three zeros.`],[5,3,`reasoning`,`A train 100 m long goes through a tunnel 100 m long. How far does it travel from when its front enters until its back comes out?`,`200 m`,[`100 m`,`50 m`,`300 m`],`The front must cross the 100 m tunnel and then go on until the whole 100 m train is out: 200 m.`],[5,3,`reasoning`,`Can you paint three faces of a cube red so that no two red faces share an edge?`,`No`,[`Yes`,`Only on a big cube`,`Only with thin paint`],`Each face touches four others; only its opposite face shares no edge with it, so at most two faces can be kept apart.`],[5,3,`riddle`,`What goes up when the rain comes down?`,`An umbrella`,[`A kite`,`A plant`,`A puddle`],`When it starts to rain, people put their umbrellas up.`],[6,1,`reasoning`,`‘All squares are rectangles.’ Which statement follows?`,`Some rectangles are squares`,[`All rectangles are squares`,`No rectangles are squares`,`Squares are not rectangles`],`If every square is a rectangle, then at least some rectangles are squares.`],[6,1,`reasoning`,`Which word can go in front of ball, print and step to make three new words?`,`Foot`,[`Hand`,`Base`,`Door`],`Football, footprint and footstep. Handball and handprint work, but not handstep.`],[6,1,`riddle`,`What is put on a table and cut, but never eaten?`,`A pack of cards`,[`A cake`,`A loaf`,`A melon`],`Before a game, a pack of cards is cut into two piles, but nobody eats it.`],[6,2,`reasoning`,`Four boxes hold 1, 2, 3 and 4 sweets, one number each. Box A has more than box B, box C has the most, and box D has 1. How many sweets are in box A?`,`3`,[`2`,`4`,`1`],`C has 4 and D has 1, so A and B have 2 and 3. A has more, so A has 3.`],[6,2,`reasoning`,`How many times must you fold a sheet of paper in half to get 8 layers?`,`3`,[`4`,`8`,`2`],`Each fold doubles the layers: 2, then 4, then 8.`],[6,2,`reasoning`,`Which word can follow sun, moon and flash to make three new words?`,`Light`,[`Shine`,`Beam`,`Rise`],`Sunlight, moonlight and flashlight.`],[6,2,`riddle`,`Which word is spelled the same forwards and backwards and names the middle of the day?`,`Noon`,[`Dusk`,`Dawn`,`Night`],`Noon is a palindrome: n-o-o-n.`],[6,3,`reasoning`,`On a standard die, opposite faces add up to 7. If the top face shows 2, what does the bottom face show?`,`5`,[`2`,`4`,`6`],`2 + 5 = 7, so 5 is underneath.`],[6,3,`reasoning`,`Sofia, Tariq and Uma each wear a different colour: red, green or blue. Sofia is not in red, and Tariq is in green. What colour is Uma wearing?`,`Red`,[`Green`,`Blue`,`Yellow`],`Tariq has green, so Sofia (not red) must wear blue, leaving red for Uma.`],[6,3,`reasoning`,`In a race, you overtake the runner who is in last place. What position are you in now?`,`It is impossible`,[`Last`,`Second to last`,`First`],`It is impossible: if someone is last, nobody is behind them to overtake them.`],[6,3,`riddle`,`Which word has three pairs of double letters, one straight after another?`,`Bookkeeper`,[`Committee`,`Balloon`,`Mississippi`],`Bookkeeper has OO, KK and EE in a row. Committee and Mississippi have double letters, but not three pairs in a row.`],[7,1,`reasoning`,`Which is the odd one out: 121, 144, 169, 172?`,`172`,[`121`,`144`,`169`],`121, 144 and 169 are square numbers (11², 12² and 13²); 172 is not.`],[7,1,`reasoning`,`8 is to 64 as 5 is to …`,`25`,[`40`,`125`,`10`],`64 is 8 squared, so the answer is 5 squared: 25.`],[7,1,`reasoning`,`Which capital letter looks the same in a mirror held beside it (has a vertical line of symmetry)?`,`A`,[`B`,`C`,`D`],`A is symmetrical left to right; B, C and D are only symmetrical top to bottom.`],[7,1,`riddle`,`What can fly without wings and cry without eyes?`,`A cloud`,[`A bird`,`A bat`,`A bee`],`Clouds drift across the sky and ‘cry’ when they rain.`],[7,2,`reasoning`,`A clock loses 5 minutes every hour. It is set right at noon. What time does it show at 6 pm?`,`5:30 pm`,[`6:30 pm`,`5:55 pm`,`5:00 pm`],`In 6 hours it loses 6 × 5 = 30 minutes, so it shows 5:30.`],[7,2,`reasoning`,`In a code, each number is a letter's position in the alphabet: 1 = A, 2 = B and so on. Which word is 8 5 12 16?`,`HELP`,[`HALP`,`HELL`,`HEMP`],`8 = H, 5 = E, 12 = L and 16 = P.`],[7,2,`reasoning`,`A rope ladder hangs over a ship's side, and 4 rungs are under water. The tide rises 60 cm. How many rungs are under water now?`,`4`,[`2`,`6`,`0`],`The ship floats, so it rises with the tide and carries the ladder up too: still 4 rungs.`],[7,3,`reasoning`,`In how many different orders can the letters of CAT be arranged?`,`6`,[`3`,`9`,`27`],`3 × 2 × 1 = 6: CAT, CTA, ACT, ATC, TCA and TAC.`],[7,3,`reasoning`,`Two candles of equal length burn down completely in 4 hours and 6 hours. Both are lit together. After how long is one exactly twice as long as the other?`,`3 hours`,[`2 hours`,`4 hours`,`5 hours`],`After 3 hours the faster candle has 1/4 left and the slower 1/2 left, which is twice as much.`],[7,3,`riddle`,`I am the beginning of eternity, the end of time and space, and the beginning of every end. What am I?`,`The letter e`,[`A clock`,`The universe`,`Zero`],`Eternity starts with e; time and space end with e; end starts with e.`],[8,1,`reasoning`,`Four people cross a bridge at night with one torch. At most two cross at a time, at the slower one's pace. They take 1, 2, 5 and 10 minutes. What is the shortest total time?`,`17 minutes`,[`19 minutes`,`18 minutes`,`20 minutes`],`1 and 2 cross (2), 1 returns (1), 5 and 10 cross (10), 2 returns (2), 1 and 2 cross (2): 17.`],[8,1,`reasoning`,`What is 1 + 2 + 3 + … + 100?`,`5,050`,[`5,000`,`10,100`,`4,950`],`Pair 1 with 100, 2 with 99 and so on: 50 pairs, each adding to 101, make 5,050.`],[8,1,`reasoning`,`What is the smallest positive whole number that every number from 1 to 10 divides exactly?`,`2,520`,[`3,628,800`,`5,040`,`1,260`],`The lowest common multiple of 1 to 10 is 2³ × 3² × 5 × 7 = 2,520.`],[8,2,`reasoning`,`Exactly one statement is true: (1) the cat is black; (2) the cat is not black; (3) the cat is white. Which statement must be false?`,`Statement 3`,[`Statement 1`,`Statement 2`,`No statement must be false`],`Statements 1 and 2 are opposites, so exactly one of them is true. That uses up the one true statement, so 3 is false.`],[8,2,`reasoning`,`In a group of 23 people, is it more likely than not that two of them share a birthday?`,`Yes, slightly more likely than not`,[`No, it needs at least 183 people`,`No, it needs 366 people`,`It is exactly 50–50`],`With 23 people there are 253 pairs, and the chance that some pair matches is about 51 %: the famous birthday problem.`],[8,2,`reasoning`,`Which of these is a perfect number, equal to the sum of its other factors?`,`28`,[`12`,`16`,`20`],`1 + 2 + 4 + 7 + 14 = 28.`],[8,2,`reasoning`,`Which figure can be drawn in one stroke without lifting the pen or going over any line twice?`,`A square with one diagonal`,[`A square with both diagonals`,`Two separate circles`,`A circle with a plus sign inside touching it`],`A figure can be drawn in one stroke only if at most two points have an odd number of lines meeting; the square with one diagonal has exactly two.`],[8,3,`reasoning`,`A two-digit number's digits add up to 9, and swapping the digits makes it 27 larger. What is the number?`,`36`,[`63`,`45`,`27`],`3 + 6 = 9, and 63 − 36 = 27.`],[8,3,`reasoning`,`Two opposite corner squares are removed from a chessboard. Can 31 dominoes, each covering two neighbouring squares, cover the remaining 62 squares?`,`No`,[`Yes`,`Only if they overlap`,`Only on a wooden board`],`Each domino covers one black and one white square, but the removed corners are the same colour, leaving 30 of one colour and 32 of the other.`],[8,3,`reasoning`,`One of three doors hides a car and the other two hide goats. You pick a door; the host, who knows where the car is, opens another to show a goat. Should you switch to the last closed door?`,`Yes: switching wins 2 times in 3`,[`No: it makes no difference`,`No: staying wins 2 times in 3`,`Yes: switching always wins`],`Your first pick is right only 1 time in 3, so the other closed door hides the car the other 2 times.`],[8,3,`riddle`,`What comes next: 1, 11, 21, 1211, 111221, …?`,`312211`,[`1112221`,`122111`,`111222`],`Each term describes the one before: 111221 is three 1s, two 2s, one 1, written 312211.`]]],i=[...t(e),...r.flatMap(e=>n(`logic`,e))];export{i as QUESTIONS};